{"parentUuid":"6bf16a27-2f7a-496a-8ef3-f798982f9fbf","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a122781128a284455","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CffqsyaCgQEMqNucX4jQK","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_0134qEmax2iuHUVAAzMe1y3A","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cat /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-nsviridenko-ws-sloprail-harness-mocks--claude-worktrees-harness-mocks-architecture-3031e7/f103aecd-8ff6-40a5-ad21-f5bd02338666/scratchpad/brief.md; cd /Users/nsviridenko/ws/sloprail/harness-mocks/.claude/worktrees/agent-a122781128a284455; git status | head -3; ls; cat spec/capabilities/noninteractive-run.yaml | head -150"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5510,"cache_read_input_tokens":45093,"output_tokens":231,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5510},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":231,"cache_read_input_tokens":45093,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5510,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5510,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_0134qEmax2iuHUVAAzMe1y3A":{"command":"cat /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-nsviridenko-ws-sloprail-harness-mocks--claude-worktrees-harness-mocks-architecture-3031e7/f103aecd-8ff6-40a5-ad21-f5bd02338666/scratchpad/brief.md; cd /Users/nsviridenko/ws/sloprail/harness-mocks/.claude/worktrees/agent-a122781128a284455; git status | head -3; ls; cat spec/capabilities/noninteractive-run.yaml | head -150"}},"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CffqsyKagKpsfHLmjd5Qb","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"684dfb21-b1d3-4f9f-8486-03c92ed3fceb","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:19:00.971Z","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/nsviridenko/ws/sloprail/harness-mocks/.claude/worktrees/agent-a122781128a284455","sessionId":"f103aecd-8ff6-40a5-ad21-f5bd02338666","version":"2.1.286","gitBranch":"worktree-agent-a8a444ee54a52b4dc"}
{"parentUuid":"684dfb21-b1d3-4f9f-8486-03c92ed3fceb","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"0e75e1c2-9f84-43c4-8006-cccad7102c77","agentId":"a122781128a284455","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0134qEmax2iuHUVAAzMe1y3A","type":"tool_result","content":"You are one of 31 parallel agents; each takes ONE capability in the repo you are in (a git worktree of sloprail/harness-mocks). Goal: every `pending` cell of your capability's spec file (spec/capabilities/<id>.yaml) becomes real: **supported** (docs + recorded runs of the REAL harness, an adapter in the mock, tests) or **unsupported with evidence**. Recorded runs outrank docs: a run showing the behaviour (even partly) => supported, the gaps listed as `deviations`; a run showing the behaviour absent (whose scenario really attempts it) => `{supported: false, reason: <one line>, runs: [...] and/or docs: [...]}`. Never leave `pending`, never a bare `false`. Do NOT change the capability's `statement` (that needs the user's words) nor other capabilities' cells.\n\nA finished example, already on main: `git show f860ff3` (codex x bash-tool-result): scenario under codex-mock/snapshots/runs/<name>/setup/, captured with the harness's snapshots/capture.sh, cell in the spec file, `// sr:provides <id>/<harness>` adapter marker, `// sr:proves <id>/<harness>` test. Read it first, plus adr/capability-grounding, adr/capability-once, adr/modeled-surface, .sloprail/schemas/capability.cue, and the existing cells and tests of the same harness.\n\nHow things work\n- Real harnesses are installed and logged in: `codex` and `cursor-agent`. Record with `<harness>-mock/snapshots/capture.sh run <name>` (the ONLY writer of snapshots/; hand-authored `setup/` = prompt.txt, hooks.json, hook scripts, optional args/env; read the script's header and the existing runs). It makes real model calls: keep prompts short, and capture only what you need (a few runs). Captures are serialised by a lock per harness; wait for it. `capture.sh doc <url>` freezes a doc page into the MANIFEST (cells cite `https://...#anchor` of a frozen page). Reuse existing runs when they already show the behaviour (cite them), else add new ones; run names are kebab-case; a new run name you create must be unique to your capability (prefix it with the capability id or a clear name) so parallel agents don't collide.\n- Look at what the real harness did (events.jsonl, stream.jsonl, payloads.jsonl, transcript/) before deciding. Your own scenario must really attempt the behaviour; if the harness cannot do it (no such tool/hook/flag), record the attempt and mark the cell unsupported citing that run (and docs if they show it).\n- Supported cell: the mock's adapter (<harness>-mock/) provides it, core behaviour lives once in internal/ (`// sr:capability <id>` already exists there; behaviour that differs per harness is a PARAMETER of core, never a copy). An adapter translates and decides nothing. Mark adapter code `// sr:provides <id>/<harness>`, and add e2e/unit tests marked `// sr:proves <id>/<harness>` (on tests only, in *_test.go) that replay/drive the mock into the recorded situation and assert what the recording shows (or the declared deviation). Unsupported cells have NO markers. `deviations` are `{adr, statement}`: use `adr: modeled-surface` where the mock models less than the harness does (interactive-only things, unimplemented tools/events), and `adr: capability-grounding` where the real harness itself doesn't do a part of the statement.\n- Scope: if modelling the behaviour in the mock is large, model what the recordings show of it and declare the rest as modeled-surface deviations; do not fake behaviour the recordings don't show.\n- CRITICAL for judges: the rules judge every capability that has a marker in any file you touch (whole file). So do NOT edit existing files that carry other capabilities' markers unless unavoidable; prefer NEW files for your adapter code and tests (new Go files must lie in a module home: see the module.yaml files / adr/modules-cover-code; internal/ and *-mock/ code each belongs to exactly one module). If you must touch a file with other markers, expect the judges to also re-judge those pairs and be ready to satisfy them. Keep `file-size` and `layering` ADR limits. Other agents work in parallel on other capabilities; touch only what yours needs; shared core edits should be small, additive, parameterised.\n- Verify: `go build ./... && go vet ./...`, tests of what you touched (`go test -count=1 ./<harness>-mock/... ./internal/... ./tools/...`; e2e suites build the mock themselves), gofmt. Then commit (signed commits are on; message body ends with `Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>`), `git fetch origin && git rebase origin/main` (conflicts, e.g. MANIFEST.yaml, are plain merges: keep both sides; if a rebase changes snapshot files, never hand-edit sealed samples), then run the repo's checks: `sr-checks run --base origin/main --head HEAD` (runs judges with a model, ~1-3 min; it caches by content; repeat until it prints no refusals). Read each refusal and fix the cause (a test that doesn't assert what the recording shows, a deviation that is false, a cell not shown by its citations...). A refusal about something clearly pre-existing and unrelated to your change: report it rather than rewriting other capabilities.\n- Merge: `git push -u origin HEAD`, `gh pr create --base main` (title \"<harness>: <id> ...\"; body ends with \"🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\"), wait for CI (`gh pr checks <n> --watch`), and when ALL checks pass: `gh pr merge <n> --squash --admin` (the user authorised admin-merge when CI passes). If main moved meanwhile and CI/verify fails on a stale base, rebase, re-run sr-checks, push --force-with-lease, and retry. Never merge with a failing check.\n- Do not touch other worktrees, never use bare `git stash`, never `cd` out of your worktree.\n\nFinal report (short): per cell: supported/unsupported, runs captured, what you implemented, PR number and merge status, anything left red and why.\n\nCapture gotcha (cursor): the installed `cursor-agent` may be newer (2026.10.01) than snapshots/MANIFEST.yaml's version (2026.09.28), and capture.sh refuses. Do NOT run `capture.sh all`. Instead put the 2026.09.28 build on PATH for the capture: look in `~/.local/share/cursor-agent/versions/` for the directory of that version and prepend it to PATH (`PATH=<dir>:$PATH cursor-mock/snapshots/capture.sh run <name>`; check `cursor-agent --version` prints 2026.09.28 first).\nOn branch worktree-agent-a122781128a284455\nnothing to commit, working tree clean\nCLA.md\nCONTRIBUTING.md\nLICENSE\nREADME.md\nadr\nclaude-mock\ncodex-mock\ncursor-mock\ngo.mod\ngo.sum\ninternal\nproposals\nspec\ntools\nstatement: >-\n  A single prompt can be run to completion without interaction, streaming one\n  machine-readable event per record and ending with one result.\nproviders:\n  claude:\n    docs:\n      - https://code.claude.com/docs/en/cli-reference#cli-flags\n      - https://code.claude.com/docs/en/headless#basic-usage\n      - https://code.claude.com/docs/en/headless#stream-responses\n    runs:\n      - claude-mock/snapshots/runs/stops\n      - claude-mock/snapshots/runs/bashfail\n  codex:\n    docs:\n      - https://developers.openai.com/codex/noninteractive#basic-usage\n      - https://developers.openai.com/codex/noninteractive#make-output-machine-readable\n    runs:\n      - codex-mock/snapshots/runs/stops\n      - codex-mock/snapshots/runs/hook-exit-codes\n      - codex-mock/snapshots/runs/prompt-blocked\n      - codex-mock/snapshots/runs/noninteractive-run-text-output\n    deviations:\n      - adr: modeled-surface\n        statement: >-\n          The mock accepts -o/--output-last-message and does nothing with it:\n          the final message is not written to a file. It calls no model and\n          has no failing turn or stream failure, so the turn.failed event and\n          the stream-level error event the docs name are not modeled. The\n          stream's item types are the agent message and the command execution\n          only: reasoning, file changes, MCP tool calls, web searches and plan\n          updates are not modeled.\n      - adr: modeled-surface\n        statement: >-\n          Without --json the mock prints Codex's progress on stderr (banner with\n          release, directory, model and session; the prompt; the notices; each\n          command with how it ended and what it printed; the agent's message;\n          the tokens used, always 0), as recorded in\n          runs/noninteractive-run-text-output. It does not print the banner's\n          provider, approval, sandbox and reasoning lines, which are\n          configuration the mock does not have, the stdin notice, or a line per\n          hook run.\n      - adr: modeled-surface\n        statement: >-\n          The mock accepts --ephemeral and does nothing with it (it always\n          writes the session's rollout file), and reads no piped stdin: a\n          prompt argument plus piped input is not combined as additional\n          context.\n  cursor:\n    docs:\n      - https://cursor.com/docs/cli/headless#how-it-works\n      - https://cursor.com/docs/cli/headless#real-time-progress-tracking\n    runs:\n      - cursor-mock/snapshots/runs/pretool-refusal\n      - cursor-mock/snapshots/runs/noninteractive-no-force\n    deviations:\n      - adr: modeled-surface\n        statement: >-\n          Without --force or --yolo the mock rejects every shell command, run\n          unprompted, and applies every file write, the two outcomes recorded\n          for a command and a write (the headless doc's file changes that are\n          only proposed were not what the recording shows). Which other\n          commands cursor-agent would run without being asked (an allowlist,\n          read-only commands) is not modeled, nor a background command's\n          rejection, which is the same as any other command's.\n      - adr: modeled-surface\n        statement: >-\n          The mock models only the stream-json output format. The docs' default\n          text format (the final answer only), the json format and\n          --stream-partial-output (partial assistant text deltas as the agent\n          writes) are not modeled: another --output-format is refused, and\n          --stream-partial-output is accepted and ignored.","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"d5e9978a-ea68-4c83-9269-2d2c40e24f73","timestamp":"2026-10-03T19:19:01.788Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"684dfb21-b1d3-4f9f-8486-03c92ed3fceb","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/nsviridenko/ws/sloprail/harness-mocks/.claude/worktrees/agent-a122781128a284455","sessionId":"f103aecd-8ff6-40a5-ad21-f5bd02338666","version":"2.1.286","gitBranch":"worktree-agent-a8a444ee54a52b4dc"}
